  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw the region $R$ and the partition $P.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/68270610704b1ef132469193e7948f54e548fa22223b6fc49b693753f998f874.png)\n\nThe upper sum $U(f,P)$ is given by\n\n$$\nU(f,P)={M}_{11}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{11})+{M}_{21}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{21})+{M}_{31}\\cdot \\text{Area}({R}_{31}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${M}_{ij}$ is the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion ${R}_{ij}.$ We pick the *maximum* value of $f$ because we're finding the *upper* sum.\n\nTaking into account the fact that $\\text{Area}({R}_{ij})=10$ for all $3$ subregions, the formula for $U(f,P)$ reduces to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10\\cdot {M}_{11}+10\\cdot {M}_{21}+10\\cdot {M}_{31} \\\\  & =10({M}_{11}+{M}_{21}+{M}_{31}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $x$ increases, and\n\n- $f(x,y)=xy$ increases as $y$ increases.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/7611c64cc460db725893156f6043644b34962a04f2aacef0b19cf99c5769cda3.png)\n\nAs a result, the *maximum* value of $f$ in each subregion is attained when we have the *largest* possible $x$ and the *largest* possible $y.$ This is the value at the *top right corner* of each subregion.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14072318/assets/75f378700ce6593a7ed9577b34e093cbbfe702e9512e9962e0d6f7f681694842.png)\n\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{11} & =f(3,6)=18 \\\\ {M}_{21} & =f(5,6)=30 \\\\ {M}_{31} & =f(7,6)=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(f,P) & =10(18+30+42) \\\\  & =900.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
